// Practice quiz: let, const and ===
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/javascript-fundamentals/let-const-and-equals

::o1 choosing a keyword::A loop counter increases on every pass. Which declaration fits? {
=let, because the name is reassigned #Correct. Reassignment is exactly what let is for.
~const, because it is the default #Not quite. Assigning to a const name throws a TypeError.
~var, because loops need function scope #Not quite. var is avoided in new code because it ignores block scope.
~No declaration, so it stays global #Not quite. Undeclared assignment is an error in strict code and a bug otherwise.
}

::o1 const by default::An array is created once and items are pushed onto it, but the name is never assigned again. Which keyword is preferred? {
=const, because the name always refers to the same array #Correct. Pushing changes the array, not the binding.
~let, because the contents change #Not quite. let is about reassigning the name, not changing contents.
~var, because arrays are objects #Not quite. var has nothing to do with objects.
~Either, since there is no difference #Not quite. There is a difference\: const tells readers the name is never reassigned.
}

::o2 const object::const cfg \= \{ retries\: 1 \}; Which line throws a TypeError? {
=cfg \= \{ retries\: 2 \}; #Correct. That reassigns the binding, which const forbids.
~cfg.retries \= 2; #Not quite. Changing a property is allowed.
~cfg.timeout \= 5; #Not quite. Adding a property is allowed.
~delete cfg.retries; #Not quite. Deleting a property is allowed on an unfrozen object.
}

::o3 loose result::What is the result of false \=\= 0? {
=true, because the boolean converts to a number first #Correct. false converts to 0, and 0 equals 0.
~false, because the types differ #Not quite. That would be the result of strict equality.
~It throws a TypeError #Not quite. Neither equality operator throws here.
~undefined #Not quite. Equality operators always produce a boolean.
}

::o3 checking for not a number::How should you test whether x holds NaN? {
=Number.isNaN(x) #Correct. NaN is not equal to itself, so a dedicated test is needed.
~x \=\=\= NaN #Not quite. This is always false, even when x is NaN.
~x \=\= NaN #Not quite. Loose equality with NaN is also always false.
~typeof x \=\=\= "NaN" #Not quite. NaN has typeof number; there is no NaN type string.
}
